"Last time you learned where to put a note on the staff to show how high it is. But a note also needs to say how long it lasts. The answer is in its shape. A note value tells us the relative length of a note by the look of its head, whether it has a stem, and whether it has flags or beams.": "532c53cccf51",
"Last time you learned where to put a note on the staff to show how high it is. But a note also needs to say how long it lasts. The answer is in its shape. A note value tells us the relative length of a note by the look of its head, whether it has a stem, and whether it has flags or beams. The idea is easy to remember: each note is half as long as the one before it. A whole note is a hollow oval with no stem. Two half notes, which have a stem, fit in the time of one whole note. Four quarter notes, with filled heads, fit in the same time. Eight eighth notes, which have one flag each, and sixteen sixteenth notes, which have two, do too. In the United States we use these names. In British English they are called semibreve, minim, crotchet, quaver, and semiquaver. How long is a quarter note in seconds? It depends on the tempo. A note value is relative. At 60 beats per minute, a quarter note lasts one second. At 120 beats per minute, it lasts half a second. In music we count beats, and a quarter note is one beat when the bottom number of the time signature is 4. So a half note gets two beats, and a whole note gets four. A dot after a note makes it longer by half of its own value. A half note is two beats, so a dotted half note is two plus one, three beats. A dotted quarter note is one and a half beats. There are rests, too. A rest is the absence of sound for a set length of time, and each kind is named for the note value it matches. A quarter rest lasts as long as a quarter note, and it is silent. Now we can understand the time signature. In 4/4, the top number says there are four beats in each bar, and the bottom number says that a quarter note is one beat. So a bar of 4/4 can hold one whole note, or two half notes, or four quarter notes, or a half note and two quarter notes. In 3/4, it holds three beats. The notes of a bar must add up exactly. That is the puzzle that composers solve in every bar. Notes with different lengths make rhythm. Taiwan’s school music also uses jianpu, the numbered notation, and it has its own signs for length. A short line after a number makes it longer, a line under a number makes it half as long, and a circle is a rest. The same ideas of halves and halves again are there too. Whichever way it is written, the relative length is what matters, and the tempo decides how many seconds it lasts.": "29c7aebb59a8",
"Notes with different lengths make rhythm. Taiwan’s school music also uses jianpu, the numbered notation, and it has its own signs for length. A short line after a number makes it longer, a line under a number makes it half as long, and a circle is a rest. The same ideas of halves and halves again are there too. Whichever way it is written, the relative length is what matters, and the tempo decides how many seconds it lasts.": "f28b3fcb98c0",
"Now we can understand the time signature. In 4/4, the top number says there are four beats in each bar, and the bottom number says that a quarter note is one beat. So a bar of 4/4 can hold one whole note, or two half notes, or four quarter notes, or a half note and two quarter notes. In 3/4, it holds three beats. The notes of a bar must add up exactly. That is the puzzle that composers solve in every bar.": "2654ab165fd3",
